{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head 353314480887a1e31927551a568b15daec8842c1 on task/release-readiness.\n\nEverything mechanical passes and the surface work is good. One claim in SECURITY.md is false about the repository it ships in, and it is the claim a security reader relies on. That is cheap to fix and expensive to publish.\n\nMUST FIX. SECURITY.md says: \"This technical preview does not yet operate a public support tracker. Security reports belong in the private channel above.\" The repository generalbusiness-ai/gitseq is already public and has issues enabled - gh api reports private false and has_issues true. A public issue tracker exists, is open, and is the first thing a visitor sees. The document's own instruction not to put a vulnerability in a public issue is aimed at a channel it simultaneously denies having.\n\nEither disable issues before the push, or reword the boundary to say that issues exist for ordinary use while security reports must go through the private advisory channel. I have no view on which; both are honest, and the choice is the owner's.\n\nThe reason this survived to review is the part worth fixing beyond the sentence. public_surface_test.go is a real gate - I mutated four things and it caught every one: dropping the SECURITY.md link from README, breaking the private-reporting link target, un-anchoring the .claude ignore pattern, and breaking a local markdown link. But it pins document CONTENT only. Nothing pins the repository's own settings, so the prose and the configuration can drift apart silently and no gate notices. A public surface that documents a support boundary should be able to fail when the boundary is not the one configured. Whether that check belongs in CI, in the push runbook, or is simply stated as a limitation is a judgement I leave to you; what I am asking is that the gap be closed or named rather than left implicit.\n\nWHAT HOLDS, checked rather than read.\n\nCI is least-privilege and pinned. permissions is contents: read at workflow scope. All three actions are pinned to commit SHAs with version comments, not floating tags. Checkout sets persist-credentials false, so the token is not left in .git/config for later steps. GOTOOLCHAIN local prevents a surprise toolchain fetch. Concurrency cancels superseded runs.\n\nThe gates run and pass at this exact head, in the reviewed checkout, with HEAD confirmed unchanged before and after: make vet, make test, make race, make build, npm test 34 of 34, make ui, make spike, and git diff --exit-code clean, which is what makes the committed UI bundle and the regenerated spike evidence honest rather than merely present.\n\nThe fresh-clone gate does what the narrow ref boundary requires, mechanically. verify-preview-clone.sh pushes only the head to refs/heads/main in a throwaway bare repo, asserts the origin's ref list is exactly refs/heads/main - no tags, no task branches, no workroom or sequence refs - clones it, and runs make test vet build using only what that clone carries, then requires a clean tree including untracked files. It passed here. That is the publication boundary proven rather than promised, and it is the strongest thing in this change.\n\nPrivate vulnerability reporting reads back enabled true from the repository, which I checked directly rather than accepting from the report.\n\nSECURITY.md is otherwise honest and under-claims where it should: it names the preview status, states the loopback-only posture, refuses response-time and compatibility commitments, and says plainly that the six adversarial cases support only the properties they name and are not a general security certification. LICENSE is MIT and the README now carries the public clone URL, the licence, the security policy link and the full local gate.\n\nOBSERVATIONS, not findings, recorded so they are not rediscovered later.\n\nSecret scanning, secret-scanning push protection, and Dependabot security updates all read back disabled. None is a stated condition and none blocks. Push protection in particular is cheap and fits this project's own stated concern about credentials never reaching the record; worth a decision before the first push rather than after.\n\nThe reachable history carries Hugh Pyle \u003chughpyle@gmail.com\u003e as author and committer throughout, alongside claude \u003cclaude@gitseq.invalid\u003e. That is the owner's own address and his to publish, but a history privacy and authorship audit should state it plainly, since publication makes it permanent and the readiness report I read does not mention it.\n\nThis branch is 14 commits behind main and main is not an ancestor. The merge result will therefore not be the tree I tested, and this approval could not cover it even if I had given one.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e86dfe2cfc2d4cfe2949e408a92ca5fb135cb732","head":"353314480887a1e31927551a568b15daec8842c1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}